From 63fb18c32a4f6d3ef2ae59bddc2d4cde65599203 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 03:59:42 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] SignalDelegator: reclaim the host frames of abandoned guest
 signal handlers

A guest handler runs on a dispatcher entered below the host frame of the
context it interrupted: StoreThreadState puts the ContextBackup under the
interrupted host SP and the dispatcher runs with SP at the backup, and
sigreturn puts the host SP back. A guest that leaves the handler with
siglongjmp or longjmp never makes that call and keeps running on the
handler's dispatcher, a few KB lower each time. Interpreters, test
harnesses, crash handlers and SIGILL feature probes all do this. After
about 2,000 abandoned handlers the host stack is gone; when the guest
stack is mapped just below the host stack the backups run into it much
sooner, which shows up as intermittent crashes and hangs. Upstream FEX
has the same structure.

Every delivery now records a handler level next to its backup (backup
address, guest frame range and host stack slot, sigaltstack range).
sigreturn pops its level, found by backup address and frame slot, with
every level inside it. A level is abandoned when the guest SP is past its
frame (checked at each delivery and at each guest syscall, which catches
sigsetjmp saving the mask while the guest is still shallow) or its frame's
host stack slot has been overwritten (the guest reused that stack). When
the innermost level is abandoned and the interrupted context is JIT code,
the dispatcher, or C++ at a drained fault-page poke, the delivery saves
the interrupted context's live host stack (SP less the red zone, up to the
abandoned dispatcher's caller-frame area), builds the new backup under the
innermost live level or the dispatcher frame, and sigreturn copies the
bytes back to the same addresses. The guest resumes on the abandoned
dispatcher; a marker record keeps that reclaimable. Other contexts, and
threads with a callback dispatcher active, nest as before. Popped levels
also release SignalHandlerRefCounter, which leaked with every abandoned
handler. The SMC fault redirect that resets SP to ReturningStackLocation
clears the markers. The design is described in SignalDelegator.cpp under
"Abandoned guest handlers".

Reproducer: a static x86-64 program that loops sigsetjmp + raise(SIGUSR1)
+ siglongjmp out of the handler 100,000 times, then 100,000 guard-page
SIGSEGVs left the same way, then 20,000 raises at a recursion depth that
changes every time. On a 64K host with FEX_HOSTPAGEMODE=force, FEX at
9da3ccf9f dies with SIGSEGV after about 2,000 handlers of the first loop;
with this change it prints raise 100000, segv 100000, deepening 20000 and
exits 0 in 2.3 s.

Ported from POWERarm (powerarm-fix/siglongjmp), where the AArch64-guest
test unittests/A64Syscalls/sys_siglongjmp covers nested handlers,
SA_NODEFER, sigaltstack, timers and threads as well.
